 "conclusions": [
  "Member queries are not reduced: about 16.4-17.0k chat-members Query per node per burst on both images (24.5 vs 25.5 per message put). The burst chat channels are two-member DMs, so each fan-out has about one recipient; the cost is the roughly eight sequential per-operation reads (action pre-check, _checkMembership, three post-persist rechecks, publish check, recipient list, notify probes), which the early start cannot combine.",
  "Burst latency regresses on accept and chat in all three fan-out runs versus all three baseline-image runs; starting membership reads concurrently with directory/lobby reads raises peak concurrent store work on the 1-CPU BFFs without removing any queries. The one soak run improved chat p95 and health but still fails; one run is not evidence of improvement.",
  "The fence argument holds: only not-yet-dispatched reads are shared (MirroredMembershipStore unchanged); each caller registers its own observer at the earlier start, so removals, epoch bumps and peer hints during the directory await deny; the directory, actor, policy and resource checks still gate the result; abandoned proofs are disposed and their rejections handled.",
  "Not a capacity improvement. The useful next step is the upstream per-operation scope (report changes #2/#3) that shares the sequential per-send reads."
 ],
